{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-130343",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=1.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-4.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A{e}^{-x}+2B{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-A+2B=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=1 \\\\ -A+2B=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=2,B=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$",
